  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A disturbance will bring occasional showers \ntoday, and widespread rain this evening.\n\nStarting the day with weak high pressure settled across the \nlower Mid-Atlantic, and a stationary front draped across the \nCarolinas. Light northeasterly winds continue to draw \nunseasonably cool moist air from New England, resulting in \nwidespread low clouds this morning.\n\nLittle change in the weather pattern will occur today, though a\nweak disturbance will pass along the Ohio River Valley during \nthe afternoon, drawing moist air from the southeast atop the \ncooler air hugging the surface in overrunning style. As such, \nspotty showers will pass across the region this morning and \nafternoon, with more widespread rain moving across the lower \nMid-Atlantic this evening. A few thunderstorms may develop with \nafternoon heating, but feel the cool air hugging the surface \nwill strictly limit the potential for storms to become severe. \nAlso noticing that abundant moisture remains just south of our\narea, which will be drawn northward across northwest North \nCarolina into the Southside area of Virginia as the disturbance \napproaches, with precipitable water rising to 2+ inches this \nevening. Winds today are forecasted to be predominantly from the\nnortheast, a direction not ideal to produce upslope, high- \nefficiency rainfall. Regardless, will have to monitor rain gages\nin the upslope areas, such as along the Blue Ridge, as Doppler \nradar does tend to underestimate upslope rainfall totals when \nwinds are generally from the Atlantic. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Cool conditions will persist through Saturday, \ngiving way to a warming trend starting Sunday.\n\nPassage of a weak disturbance east tonight will nudge the front\nacross the Carolinas a little further south into Saturday \nmorning, with drier air arriving from the Great Lakes region. \nThis will result in overall drier conditions across the lower \nMid-Atlantic for Saturday and Sunday, with only disorganized, \nisolated to widely scattered showers and thunderstorms \ndeveloping with afternoon heating. \n\nTemperatures will remain unseasonably cool on Saturday before \nreturning to near normal on Sunday, the start of a warming trend\nahead of another cold front approaching from the Great Lakes. \nAs deeper moisture pools south of this front, we can expect the \nreturn of thunderstorms for Monday through Wednesday that will \nbe capable of heavy downpours that could result in localized \nflash flooding. If the front can advance this far south, it will\ndo so most likely on Wednesday night, and will have the \npotential of bringing another surge of dry Canadian air for the \nsecond half of the workweek.",
